    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a faithful structural mirror of the existing guard trio in this codebase (`CheckOwnerCityCapacity` / `CityAtCapacityException` / `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`):\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** `CheckDailyOwnerLimit` is a single-method guard function sitting alongside the other `Check*` guards, wired as its own pipeline step before `build` \u2014 exactly where `checkCityCapacity` sits. The guard throws; the escalation handler responds. No responsibility is misplaced.\n- **No catch-all growth.** Each concern gets its own small class (function, checked exception, handler) rather than being bolted onto an existing one, matching the per-concern decomposition already in `function/owner/` and `escalation/`.\n- **Reuse is idiomatic.** The handler delegates to the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper just like every sibling handler, and the `@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerRepository` / `findAll().stream()...count()` shape, the `DAILY_LIMIT` constant, and the Javadoc style all match `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` line-for-line.\n- **Conventions followed.** Checked exception (so it appears in `throws` and routes to escalation), escalation YAML named by FQCN, and the `next:` wiring is additive and ordered consistently with the existing guard chain.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this entirely natural \u2014 it looks like it was written by the same hand as the city-capacity guard.",
